     "type": "practice",
     "title": "Practice 1: Build Your Daily Ego Audit Now",
     "content": "**Time: 30 minutes to set up. Then 5 minutes/day forever.**\n\nWrite this audit as a template you will use every day for the next 90 days.\n\n**Step 1 — Choose your trigger.**\nWhen will you run the audit? Common options:\n• Right after waking, before phone\n• Last thing before bed\n• During your evening walk\n• At a specific time (e.g., 9pm, with a phone alarm)\n\nPick one. Commit. Same trigger every day for the 90 days.\n\n**Step 2 — Choose your medium.**\n• Paper notebook (most resistant to distractions)\n• Single text file you append to\n• Dedicated note-taking app section\n\nDon't overthink. Pick one. The medium matters less than the consistency.\n\n**Step 3 — Write the six questions on the first page.**\n\n1. *Where did ego whisper today?* (A snap, a brag, a skipped rep, a dismissed feedback)\n2. *Did I execute my Standard?* (Specific yes/no on key items)\n3. *What did I learn — from whom?* (Plus, Minus, or Equal — one name)\n4. *Was there flattery I absorbed?* (Calibrated praise, noted and calibrated)\n5. *What was alive time? What was dead?* (Even 1 hour — name it)\n6. *Who did I not thank?* (Whose unseen work made today possible)\n\n**Step 4 — Write today's first entry.**\nDon't wait. Right now, answer all six for today. The first entry is always awkward. Do it anyway — the awkwardness disappears by day 7.\n\n**Step 5 — Set a 30-day check-in.**\nIn 30 days, re-read your last 30 entries. Patterns will emerge. The patterns are the data ego has been hiding from you.\n\nThis is the most important practice of the seminar. **If you only keep one thing, keep this.**",
