     "type": "example",
     "title": "A Real Tension: Does Anti-Ego Advice Keep Ambitious People Small?",
     "content": "Popularizing Stoic self-restraint at scale raises a legitimate critique that deserves to be taken as seriously as the philosophy itself. A recurring argument, made across discussions of workplace equity and voiced especially by writers examining the experiences of women, first-generation professionals, and people from marginalized racial or economic backgrounds, is that advice to be humble, defer credit, avoid self-promotion, and subordinate one's ego is not neutral in its effects. It lands very differently depending on who receives it. A person who already occupies a position of structural advantage can afford to underplay their achievements, because the surrounding system will often notice and reward them anyway, through informal networks, assumed competence, or the benefit of the doubt. A person without that structural advantage, who is statistically less likely to be given credit automatically, less likely to be assumed competent by default, and more likely to face real penalties for the same self-assertive behavior that reads as confidence in someone else, may find that identical anti-ego advice functions less like wisdom and more like a tool that keeps them artificially small, quiet, and passed over. This is a genuine tension rather than a simple refutation: Holiday's diagnosis of ego as self-sabotage is well supported by real cases, including several examined earlier in this book, yet the same advice, applied without attention to context, can reinforce exactly the inequities that make some people's ambition already harder to sustain than others'. The honest position is not to discard the book's insights, but to recognize that humility is a tool suited to specific failure modes, entitlement, deafness to feedback, self-mythologizing, and not a universal prescription that applies identically regardless of a person's starting position.",
